    n<// Memory Map:> u<9335> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<20706> s<9336> l<1239:1> el<1239:15>
    n<//> u<9336> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<20706> s<9337> l<1240:1> el<1240:3>
    n<// PIC Data Memory addressing is complicated.  See the Data Book for full explanation..> u<9337> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<20706> s<9338> l<1241:1> el<1241:88>
    n<//> u<9338> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<20706> s<9339> l<1242:1> el<1242:3>
    n<// Basically, each BANK contains 32 locations.  The lowest 16 locations in ALL Banks> u<9339> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<20706> s<9340> l<1243:1> el<1243:85>
    n<// are really all mapped to the same bank (bank #0).  The first 8 locations are the Special> u<9340> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<20706> s<9341> l<1244:1> el<1244:92>
    n<// registers like the STATUS and PC registers.  The upper 16 words in each bank, really are> u<9341> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<20706> s<9342> l<1245:1> el<1245:92>
    n<// unique to each bank.  The smallest PIC (16C54) only has the one bank #0.> u<9342> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<20706> s<9343> l<1246:1> el<1246:76>
    n<//> u<9343> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<20706> s<9344> l<1247:1> el<1247:3>
    n<// So, as a programmer, what you get is this.  No matter what bank you are in (FSR[6:5])> u<9344> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<20706> s<9345> l<1248:1> el<1248:89>
    n<// you always have access to your special registers and also to registers 8-15.  You can> u<9345> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<20706> s<9346> l<1249:1> el<1249:89>
    n<// change to a 1 of 4 banks by setting FSR[6:5] and get 4 different sets of registers> u<9346> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<20706> s<9347> l<1250:1> el<1250:86>
    n<// 16-31.> u<9347> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<20706> s<9348> l<1251:1> el<1251:10>
    n<//> u<9348> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<20706> s<9349> l<1252:1> el<1252:3>
    n<// For numbering purposes, I've numbered the registers as if they are one linear memory> u<9349> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<20706> s<9350> l<1253:1> el<1253:88>
    n<// space, just like in the Data Book (see Figure 4-15 "Direct/Indirect Addressing").> u<9350> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<20706> s<9351> l<1254:1> el<1254:85>
    n<// So, the unique 16 registers in bank #1 are named r48 - r63 (I use decimal).  The> u<9351> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<20706> s<9352> l<1255:1> el<1255:84>
    n<// unique registers in bank #3 are therefore r112 - r127.  There is no r111 because,> u<9352> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<20706> s<9353> l<1256:1> el<1256:85>
    n<// remember, the lower 16 registers each each bank are all reall the same registers 0-15.> u<9353> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<20706> s<9354> l<1257:1> el<1257:90>
    n<// > u<9354> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<20706> s<9355> l<1258:1> el<1258:4>
    n<// Confused?!  The Data Book explains it better than I can.> u<9355> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<20706> s<9356> l<1259:1> el<1259:60>
